    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d918">

                <pb facs="1047" n="954" sameAs="frus:frus1935v04/pg954"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">633.113/10</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Uruguay</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Lay</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 17</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-08-13">August 13, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: There is enclosed a copy of a letter, dated
                    August 5, 1935,<anchor n="7" xml:id="d918-f1"/> received from the American
                    Manufacturers Export Association, of 330 West Forty-second Street, New York, New
                    York, reporting a rumor that the Uruguayan Government has increased customs
                    duties on all articles imported from the United States by 30%, whereas for other
                    countries the duties were raised by only 15%.</p>
                <p>You are requested to investigate the matter and if your investigation shows that
                    there is customs discrimination against the United <pb facs="1048" n="955"
                        xml:id="pg955"/> States, you are authorized to take the matter up with the
                    Uruguayan Minister for Foreign Affairs and ask that the discrimination be
                    removed.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sumner
                            Welles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
